+# Agent Note: Trusted proxy authentication
+
+Status: implemented
+
+English | [中文](2026-10-12-trusted-proxy-authentication.zh.md)
+
+## Problem
+
+A deployment protected by an external identity provider otherwise requires a second browser enrollment using a launch token from the host journal. Retaining a separate dsh cookie as an alternative admission path would weaken the deployment's single sign-on policy.
+
+## Decision
+
+Connection supports an explicit, exclusive proxy mode selected by a private key-file path. The authenticating proxy inserts a per-deployment 256-bit credential only after user authorization. Dsh validates it in constant time for each request and upgrade, while retaining browser-trust checks. Index requests receive the same trust checks. Proxy mode neither reads browser-session credentials nor mints a launch token or browser cookie.
+
+## Alternatives considered
+
+**Disable authentication on loopback.** Other local processes could then act as the operator after a network-policy mistake. A separate proxy key preserves an application-level check.
+
+**Harvest the launch token from logs.** This couples access to output formatting and retains two independently managed browser sessions. Exclusive proxy authentication directly expresses the deployment's access policy.
+
+## Consequences
+
+A leaked proxy key permits operator access wherever the backend is reachable. Private runtime files, public-header removal, post-authorization injection, and local network restrictions are mandatory deployment controls. The proxy owns user authorization, logout, and session refresh; established streams are not retroactively revoked. Rotation requires both Connection activation and proxy reload. The default local browser-token mode remains available when no proxy key file is configured.
